var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"research/#Research-and-Publications","page":"Research & Publications","text":"LaplacianOpt is developed alongside research on mathematical optimization methods for maximizing graph algebraic connectivity and designing robust networks. Select a publication to read a short overview and follow its paper links or copy its BibTeX citation. If you find LaplacianOpt useful, we request that you cite the papers most relevant to your work.\n\n<section class=\"publication-timeline\" aria-label=\"LaplacianOpt publication timeline\">\n  <details class=\"publication-card\">\n    <summary>\n      <span class=\"publication-year\">2024</span>\n      <span class=\"publication-heading\">\n        <span class=\"publication-title\">Optimal Robust Network Design: Formulations and Algorithms for Maximizing Algebraic Connectivity</span>\n        <span class=\"publication-venue\">IEEE Transactions on Control of Network Systems</span>\n      </span>\n    </summary>\n    <div class=\"publication-body\">\n      <p class=\"publication-authors\">Neelkamal Somisetty, Harsha Nagarajan, and Swaroop Darbha</p>\n      <p>\n        This work develops exact and scalable optimization methods for designing\n        edge-weighted networks with maximum algebraic connectivity. It presents\n        mixed-integer semidefinite and linear formulations, principal-minor\n        upper bounds, degree-constrained lower bounds, and a maximum-cost\n        heuristic for larger networks.\n      </p>\n      <nav class=\"publication-links\" aria-label=\"Links for the TCNS 2024 publication\">\n        <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TCNS.2024.3431408\">IEEE</a>\n        <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.08571\">arXiv</a>\n      </nav>\n      <div class=\"citation-block\">\n        <div class=\"citation-header\">\n          <span>BibTeX</span>\n          <button type=\"button\" class=\"bibtex-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy the TCNS 2024 BibTeX citation\">Copy BibTeX</button>\n        </div>\n        <pre><code class=\"language-bibtex\">@article{LOpt_TCNS2024,\n  title={Optimal Robust Network Design: Formulations and Algorithms for Maximizing Algebraic Connectivity},\n  author={Somisetty, Neelkamal and Nagarajan, Harsha and Darbha, Swaroop},\n  journal={IEEE Transactions on Control of Network Systems},\n  pages={918--929},\n  volume={12},\n  number={1},\n  year={2024},\n  doi={10.1109/TCNS.2024.3431408},\n  publisher={IEEE}\n}</code></pre>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </details>\n\n  <details class=\"publication-card\">\n    <summary>\n      <span class=\"publication-year\">2024</span>\n      <span class=\"publication-heading\">\n        <span class=\"publication-title\">Spectral Graph Theoretic Methods for Enhancing Network Robustness in Robot Localization</span>\n        <span class=\"publication-venue\">63rd IEEE Conference on Decision and Control (CDC)</span>\n      </span>\n    </summary>\n    <div class=\"publication-body\">\n      <p class=\"publication-authors\">Neelkamal Somisetty, Harsha Nagarajan, and Swaroop Darbha</p>\n      <p>\n        Motivated by cooperative robot localization and pose-graph\n        sparsification, this paper strengthens algebraic-connectivity models\n        with Cheeger cuts. It develops a mixed-integer linear formulation for\n        computing Cheeger constants, incorporates the cuts into an\n        outer-approximation algorithm, and introduces a greedy k-opt heuristic.\n      </p>\n      <nav class=\"publication-links\" aria-label=\"Links for the CDC 2024 publication\">\n        <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/CDC56724.2024.10886863\">IEEE</a>\n        <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.15506\">arXiv</a>\n      </nav>\n      <div class=\"citation-block\">\n        <div class=\"citation-header\">\n          <span>BibTeX</span>\n          <button type=\"button\" class=\"bibtex-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy the CDC 2024 BibTeX citation\">Copy BibTeX</button>\n        </div>\n        <pre><code class=\"language-bibtex\">@inproceedings{LOpt_CDC2024,\n  title={Spectral Graph Theoretic Methods for Enhancing Network Robustness in Robot Localization},\n  author={Somisetty, Neelkamal and Nagarajan, Harsha and Darbha, Swaroop},\n  booktitle={IEEE 63rd Conference on Decision and Control (CDC)},\n  pages={8811--8818},\n  year={2024},\n  doi={10.1109/CDC56724.2024.10886863},\n  organization={IEEE}\n}</code></pre>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </details>\n\n  <details class=\"publication-card\">\n    <summary>\n      <span class=\"publication-year\">2015</span>\n      <span class=\"publication-heading\">\n        <span class=\"publication-title\">On Maximizing Algebraic Connectivity of Networks for Various Engineering Applications</span>\n        <span class=\"publication-venue\">European Control Conference (ECC)</span>\n      </span>\n    </summary>\n    <div class=\"publication-body\">\n      <p class=\"publication-authors\">Harsha Nagarajan, Sivakumar Rathinam, and Swaroop Darbha</p>\n      <p>\n        This paper studies algebraic connectivity as a robustness objective for\n        mechanical systems, vehicle localization, UAV formations, and\n        communication networks. It also develops a Fiedler-vector-based\n        mixed-integer linear formulation and cutting-plane methods for finding\n        optimal solutions and upper bounds.\n      </p>\n      <nav class=\"publication-links\" aria-label=\"Links for the ECC 2015 publication\">\n        <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/ECC.2015.7330770\">IEEE</a>\n      </nav>\n      <div class=\"citation-block\">\n        <div class=\"citation-header\">\n          <span>BibTeX</span>\n          <button type=\"button\" class=\"bibtex-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy the ECC 2015 BibTeX citation\">Copy BibTeX</button>\n        </div>\n        <pre><code class=\"language-bibtex\">@inproceedings{LOpt_ECC2015,\n  title={On Maximizing Algebraic Connectivity of Networks for Various Engineering Applications},\n  author={Nagarajan, Harsha and Rathinam, Sivakumar and Darbha, Swaroop},\n  booktitle={European Control Conference (ECC)},\n  pages={1626--1632},\n  year={2015},\n  doi={10.1109/ECC.2015.7330770},\n  organization={IEEE}\n}</code></pre>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </details>\n</section>","title":"Research & Publications"},{"category":"section","location":"quickguide/#Quick-Start-Guide","page":"Quick Start guide","text":"","title":"Quick Start Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"quickguide/#Framework","page":"Quick Start guide","text":"Building on Julia, JuMP, and mixed-integer programming (MIP) solvers, LaplacianOpt is an open-source toolkit for maximum algebraic connectivity augmentation on graphs. As illustrated below, LaplacianOpt is written in Julia, a fast programming language for technical computing with an extensible type system and metaprogramming support. At a high level, this package provides an abstraction layer to achieve two primary goals:\n\nTo capture user-specified inputs, such as the number of vertices of the graph, adjacency matrices of existing and augmentation graphs, and an augmentation budget, and to build a JuMP model of an MIP formulation with convex relaxations, and \nTo extract, analyze and post-process the solution from the JuMP model and to provide optimal connected graphs with maximum algebraic connectivity.\n\n<align=\"center\"/>\n<img width=\"550px\" class=\"display-light-only\" src=\"../assets/LOpt_framework.png\" alt=\"../assets/LOpt_framework.png\"/>","title":"Framework"},{"category":"section","location":"quickguide/#Getting-started","page":"Quick Start guide","text":"After installing LaplacianOpt and a MIP solver such as Gurobi.jl or the open-source GLPK, provide inputs based on the available graph data. LaplacianOpt accepts either JSON input or a data dictionary. The following example uses a data dictionary; see the example script for JSON input.\n\nimport LaplacianOpt as LOpt\nusing JuMP\nusing Gurobi\n\nfunction data()\n    data_dict = Dict{String, Any}()\n    data_dict[\"num_nodes\"] = 4\n\n    # Base graph with 3 existing edges (fixed). Note this graph can also be empty. \n    data_dict[\"adjacency_base_graph\"] = [0 2 0 0; 2 0 3 0; 0 3 0 4; 0 0 4 0]\n\n    # Augmentation graph with 3 candidate edges\n    data_dict[\"adjacency_augment_graph\"] = [0 0 4 8; 0 0 0 7; 4 0 0 0; 8 7 0 0]\n\n    # Augmentation budget on candidate edges\n    augment_budget = 2\n    return data_dict, augment_budget\nend\n\ndata_dict, augment_budget = data()\n\nparams = Dict{String, Any}(\n    \"data_dict\"           => data_dict,\n    \"augment_budget\"      => augment_budget\n    )\n\nlopt_optimizer = JuMP.optimizer_with_attributes(Gurobi.Optimizer, \"presolve\" => 1) \nresults = LOpt.run_LOpt(params, lopt_optimizer)\n\ntip: Tip\nRun times of LaplacianOpt's mathematical optimization models are significantly faster using Gurobi as the underlying mixed-integer programming (MIP) solver. Note that this solver's individual-usage license is available free for academic purposes. \n\ntip: Tip\nLaplacianOpt solves a combinatorial optimization problem that is known to be NP-hard in num_nodes. To obtain quick feasible solutions using k-opt-based heuristics, set :solution_type to \"heuristic\" in the model options and adjust :num_swaps_bound_kopt. A larger value can improve solution quality at the cost of increased heuristic runtime.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"quickguide/#Extracting-results","page":"Quick Start guide","text":"For an optimal run, run_LOpt returns detailed results as a dictionary. For example, the runtime and optimal objective value (maximum algebraic connectivity) can be accessed with\n\nresults[\"solve_time\"]\nresults[\"objective\"]\n\nThe \"solution\" field contains detailed information about the solution produced by the optimization model. For example, the edges of the optimal graph topology can be obtained from the symmetric adjacency matrix with\n\noptimal_graph = LOpt.optimal_graph_edges(results[\"solution\"][\"z_var\"])\n\nFurther, algebraic connectivity and the Fiedler vector of the optimal graph topology (though can be applied on any graph topology) can be obtained from the adjacency matrix with,\n\ndata = LOpt.get_data(params)\nadjacency_matrix = data[\"adjacency_base_graph\"] + data[\"adjacency_augment_graph\"]\noptimal_adjacency = results[\"solution\"][\"z_var\"] .* adjacency_matrix \ngraph_data = LOpt.GraphData(optimal_adjacency)\nprintln(\"Algebraic connectivity: \", graph_data.ac)\nprintln(\"Fiedler vector: \", graph_data.fiedler)","title":"Extracting results"},{"category":"section","location":"quickguide/#Visualizing-results","page":"Quick Start guide","text":"LaplacianOpt supports visualizing optimal graph layouts obtained from the results dictionary. Two options are available:\n\nTikzGraphs provides a simple graph layout with optional edge weights:\n\ndata = LOpt.get_data(params)\nLOpt.visualize_solution(results, data, visualizing_tool = \"tikz\")\n\nGraphviz provides visualization of weighted graphs. LaplacianOpt generates a raw .dot file that can be rendered using a local Graphviz installation or an online frontend such as Edotor:\n\ndata = LOpt.get_data(params)\nLOpt.visualize_solution(results, data, visualizing_tool = \"graphviz\")\n\nFor example, for the weighted complete graph with 10 nodes in instance #1, the optimal spanning tree with maximum algebraic connectivity, selected from 10^8 feasible solutions and visualized using Graphviz, is shown below.\n\n(Image: Optimal solution)","title":"Visualizing results"},{"category":"section","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.jl-Function-References","page":"Function References","text":"","title":"LaplacianOpt.jl Function References"},{"category":"type","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.GraphData","page":"Function References","text":"GraphData\n\nThe composite mutable struct, GraphData, holds matrices of adjacency matrix,  laplacian matrix, fiedler vector and algebraic connectivity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.GraphData"},{"category":"type","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.LaplacianOptModel","page":"Function References","text":"LaplacianOptModel\n\nThe composite mutable struct, LaplacianOptModel, holds dictionaries for input data, abstract JuMP model for optimization, variable references and result from solving the JuMP model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.LaplacianOptModel"},{"category":"type","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.LaplacianOptModelOptions","page":"Function References","text":"LaplacianOptModelOptions\n\nThe composite mutable struct, LaplacianOptModelOptions, holds various optimization  model options for enhancements with default options set to the values provided by get_default_options function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.LaplacianOptModelOptions"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt._PMinorIdx-Tuple{Int64, Vector{Int64}, Bool, Dict{String, Any}}","page":"Function References","text":"_PMinorIdx(\n    N::Int64,\n    sizes::Vector{Int64},\n    minors_on_augment_edges::Bool,\n    data::Dict{String,Any},\n)\n\nGenerates a dictionary mapping minor sizes to their corresponding vertex tuples. For each size k in the input sizes vector, creates tuples of k vertices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt._PMinorIdx"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt._catch_data_input_error-Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64, Number}","page":"Function References","text":"_catch_data_input_error(num_nodes::Int64, i::Int64, j::Int64, w_ij::Number)\n\nGiven the number of nodes, from and to nodes of an edge, and an edge weight, this function catches  any input data error in the JSON file. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt._catch_data_input_error"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt._detect_infeasbility_in_data-Tuple{Dict{String, Any}}","page":"Function References","text":"_detect_infeasbility_in_data(data::Dict{String, Any})\n\nGiven the pre-processed data dictionary, this function detects any infeasibility before  building the optimization model. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt._detect_infeasbility_in_data"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt._is_flow_cut_valid-Tuple{Vector{Int64}, Vector{Int64}, Int64, Array{<:Number}}","page":"Function References","text":"_is_flow_cut_valid(cutset_f::Vector{Int64}, cutset_t::Vector{Int64}, adjacency::Array{<:Number})\n\nGiven from and to sets of vertices of connected components  of a graph, this function returns a boolean if the input  num_edges number of candidate edge exist to augment or not,  between these two sets of vertices. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt._is_flow_cut_valid"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt._violated_eigen_vector-Tuple{Array{<:Number}}","page":"Function References","text":"_violated_eigen_vector(W::Array{<:Number}; tol = 1E-6)\n\nGiven a square symmetric matrix, this function returns an eigen vector corresponding to it's most  violated eigen value w.r.t positive semi-definiteness of the input matrix.  \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt._violated_eigen_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.algebraic_connectivity-Tuple{Array{<:Number}}","page":"Function References","text":"algebraic_connectivity(adjacency_matrix::Array{<:Number})\n\nReturns the algebraic connectivity or the   second smallest eigenvalue of the Laplacian matrix,  for an input weighted adjacency matrix of the graph. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.algebraic_connectivity"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.cheeger_constant-Tuple{Matrix{<:Number}, MathOptInterface.OptimizerWithAttributes}","page":"Function References","text":"cheeger_constant(G::Matrix{<:Number}, \n                 optimizer::MOI.OptimizerWithAttributes; \n                 cheeger_ub = 1E4,\n                 formulation_type = \"set_cardinality\",\n                 optimizer_log = false)\n\nGiven a symmetric, weighted adjacency matrix G of a connected graph, and a mixed-integer linear optimizer,  this function returns the Cheeger's constant (or isoperimetric number of the graph) and the associated two partitions  (S and S_complement) of the graph. \n\nThe exact version of the formulation implemented here is published in: Somisetty, N., Nagarajan, H. and Darbha, S., 2024. \"Spectral Graph Theoretic Methods for Enhancing Network Robustness  in Robot Localization,\" IEEE 63rd Conference on Decision and Control (CDC), 2024.  Link: https://doi.org/10.1109/CDC56724.2024.10886863\n\nReferences:  (I) Chung, F.R., \"Laplacians of graphs and Cheeger's inequalities\", Combinatorics, Paul Erdos is Eighty, 2(157-172), pp.13-2, 1996. (II) Mohar, B., 1989. Isoperimetric numbers of graphs. Journal of combinatorial theory,  Series B, 47(3), pp.274-291. Link: https://doi.org/10.1016/0095-8956(89)90029-4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.cheeger_constant"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.edge_combinations-Tuple{Int64, Int64}","page":"Function References","text":"edge_combinations(n::Int64, k::Int64)\n\nReturns all combinations possible for given n and k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.edge_combinations"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.fiedler_vector-Tuple{Array{<:Number}}","page":"Function References","text":"fiedler_vector(adjacency_matrix::Array{<:Number})\n\nReturns the Fiedler vector or the eigenvector corresponding to the  second smallest eigenvalue of the Laplacian matrix for an input  weighted adjacency matrix of the graph. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.fiedler_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.get_data-Tuple{Dict{String, Any}}","page":"Function References","text":"get_data(params::Dict{String, Any})\n\nGiven the input params, this function preprocesses the data, catches any error and missing data,  and outputs a detailed dictionary which forms the basis for building an optimization model. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.get_data"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.get_default_options-Tuple{}","page":"Function References","text":"get_default_options()\n\nThis function returns the default options for building the struct LaplacianOptModelOptions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.get_default_options"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.get_minor_idx-Tuple{Vector{Int64}, Int64}","page":"Function References","text":"get_minor_idx(vector::Vector{Int64}, size::Int64)\n\nGiven a vector and the desired size of tuples, this  recursive function outputs all possible tuples of the  specified size from the elements of the vector.  \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.get_minor_idx"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.get_objective_bound-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Function References","text":"\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.get_objective_bound"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.get_objective_value-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"Function References","text":"\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.get_objective_value"},{"category":"function","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.get_unique_cycles","page":"Function References","text":"get_unique_cycles(G::Graphs.SimpleGraphs.SimpleGraph{<:Number}, \n    max_cycle_length::Int, \n    min_cycle_length = 3, \n    ceiling = 100)\n\nGiven an undirected graph of type Graphs.SimpleGraphs,  this function returns unique cycles of maximum length given by max_cycle_length. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.get_unique_cycles"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.laplacian_matrix-Tuple{Array{<:Number}}","page":"Function References","text":"laplacian_matrix(adjacency_matrix::Array{<:Number})\n\nGiven a weighted adjacency matrix as an input, this function returns the  weighted Laplacian matrix of the graph. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.laplacian_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.logger_config!-Tuple{Base.CoreLogging.LogLevel}","page":"Function References","text":"allows the user to set the logging level\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.logger_config!"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.optimal_graph_edges-Tuple{Array{<:Number}}","page":"Function References","text":"optimal_graph_edges(adjacency_matrix::Array{<:Number})\n\nReturns a vector of tuples of edges corresponding  to an input adjacency matrix of the graph. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.optimal_graph_edges"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.optimality_certificate_MISDP-Tuple{LaplacianOptModel, Dict{String, Any}}","page":"Function References","text":"optimality_certificate_MISDP(lom::LaplacianOptModel, result::Dict{String,Any})\n\nGiven the LaplacianOpt model (lom) and the results dictionary, this function returns  a boolean if the integer solution satisfies the optimality certificate for the MISDP problem. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.optimality_certificate_MISDP"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.priority_central_nodes-Tuple{Array{<:Number}, Int64}","page":"Function References","text":"priority_central_nodes(adjacency_augment_graph::Array{<:Number}, num_nodes::Int64)\n\nReturns nodes sorted by their centrality, measured as the sum of edge weights  connected to each node, in descending order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.priority_central_nodes"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.relaxation_bilinear-Tuple{JuMP.Model, JuMP.VariableRef, JuMP.VariableRef, JuMP.VariableRef}","page":"Function References","text":"relaxation_bilinear(m::JuMP.Model, xy::JuMP.VariableRef, x::JuMP.VariableRef, y::JuMP.VariableRef)\n\nGeneral relaxation of a binlinear term using McCormick relaxations. This can be used to obtain specific variants  in partiuclar cases of variables (when either or both the variables are binary)\n\nz >= JuMP.lower_bound(x)*y + JuMP.lower_bound(y)*x - JuMP.lower_bound(x)*JuMP.lower_bound(y)\nz >= JuMP.upper_bound(x)*y + JuMP.upper_bound(y)*x - JuMP.upper_bound(x)*JuMP.upper_bound(y)\nz <= JuMP.lower_bound(x)*y + JuMP.upper_bound(y)*x - JuMP.lower_bound(x)*JuMP.upper_bound(y)\nz <= JuMP.upper_bound(x)*y + JuMP.lower_bound(y)*x - JuMP.upper_bound(x)*JuMP.lower_bound(y)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.relaxation_bilinear"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.round_zeros_ones!-Tuple{Array{<:Number}}","page":"Function References","text":"round_zeros_ones!(v::Array{<:Number}; tol = 1E-6)\n\nGiven a vector of numbers, this function updates the input vector by  rounding the values closest to 0, 1 and -1. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.round_zeros_ones!"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.silence-Tuple{}","page":"Function References","text":"Suppresses information and warning messages output by LaplacianOpt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.silence"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.variable_domain-Tuple{JuMP.VariableRef}","page":"Function References","text":"variable_domain(var::JuMP.VariableRef)\n\nComputes the valid domain of a given JuMP variable taking into account bounds and the varaible's implicit bounds (e.g. binary).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.variable_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"function_references/#LaplacianOpt.weighted_adjacency_matrix-Tuple{Graphs.SimpleGraphs.SimpleGraph{<:Number}, Array{<:Number}, Int64}","page":"Function References","text":"weighted_adjacency_matrix(G::Graphs.SimpleGraphs.SimpleGraph{<:Number}, adjacency_augment_graph::Array{<:Number}, size::Int64)\n\nReturns a weighted adjacency matrix for  the connected part of graph.  \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LaplacianOpt.weighted_adjacency_matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"#LaplacianOpt-Documentation","page":"Introduction","text":"","title":"LaplacianOpt Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Introduction","text":"LaplacianOpt is a Julia package that implements polyhedral relaxation-based algorithms for the maximum algebraic connectivity augmentation problem on weighted graph Laplacians. More specifically, given a weighted base graph with existing edges (which could be empty), a set of candidate weighted edges for augmentation, and an augmentation budget (K), this package finds a set of K edges to augment the base graph such that the resulting graph has maximum algebraic connectivity with optimality guarantees. For example, given a base graph with N vertices and no edges, a set of candidate edges that forms a complete graph, and K = N - 1, this package finds a spanning tree with maximum algebraic connectivity.\n\nAlgebraic connectivity is the second smallest eigenvalue of the graph Laplacian. The magnitude of this value reflects how well connected the overall graph is. This connectivity measure has been used in analyzing the robustness and synchronizability of complex networks, and in graph sparsification techniques. ","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Introduction","text":"To use LaplacianOpt, first download and install Julia. The current version of LaplacianOpt requires Julia 1.10 or later.\n\nThe latest stable release of LaplacianOpt can be installed using the Julia package manager with\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.add(\"LaplacianOpt\")\n\nAt least one mixed-integer programming solver is required to run LaplacianOpt. CPLEX and Gurobi are recommended for solving larger graph instances. The open-source GLPK solver is also compatible with LaplacianOpt and can be installed through the package manager with\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.add(\"GLPK\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Unit-Tests","page":"Introduction","text":"To run the tests in the package, run the following command after installing the LaplacianOpt package.\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.test(\"LaplacianOpt\")","title":"Unit Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing-LaplacianOpt","page":"Introduction","text":"If you find LaplacianOpt useful in your work, please cite the publication(s) most relevant to your use of the package. The Research & Publications page provides a chronological overview of the research, paper links, and copyable BibTeX citations.","title":"Citing LaplacianOpt"}]
}
